# Interfaces vs Type Aliases

Both allow you to define the shape of an object.

### Interfaces
Can be merged if defined multiple times.
```typescript
interface User {
  name: string;
}
interface User {
  age: number;
}
```

### Type Aliases
More flexible, can define primitives, unions, and tuples.
```typescript
type Point = { x: number; y: number };
type ID = string | number;
```

# Generics

Create reusable components that work with a variety of types.

```typescript
function identity<T>(arg: T): T {
  return arg;
}

let output = identity<string>("myString");
```

Generics can also be used in interfaces and classes to ensure type safety while maintaining flexibility.

# Union & Intersection Types

### Union Types
A value can be one of several types.
```typescript
function printId(id: number | string) {
  console.log("ID is: " + id);
}
```

### Intersection Types
Combines multiple types into one.
```typescript
interface ErrorHandling {
  success: boolean;
  error?: { message: string };
}

interface ArtworksData {
  artworks: { title: string }[];
}

type ArtworksResponse = ArtworksData & ErrorHandling;
```

# Access Modifiers

TypeScript supports `public`, `private`, and `protected` modifiers for class members.

- `public`: (Default) Accessible anywhere.
- `private`: Accessible only within the class.
- `protected`: Accessible within the class and its subclasses.

```typescript
class Animal {
  private name: string;
  constructor(theName: string) { this.name = theName; }
}
```

# Abstract Classes

Base classes from which other classes may be derived. They may not be instantiated directly.

```typescript
abstract class Shape {
  abstract getArea(): number;
  print() { console.log("Printing shape..."); }
}

class Circle extends Shape {
  constructor(public radius: number) { super(); }
  getArea() { return Math.PI * this.radius ** 2; }
}
```

# Modules

TypeScript uses the ES6 module syntax (`import`/`export`). 

```typescript
// math.ts
export function add(x: number, y: number) { return x + y; }

// main.ts
import { add } from "./math";
```
Modules are executed within their own scope, not in the global scope.
